{"reference":"1 Chronicles 1","book":"1 Chronicles","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Genealogy","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"This chapter opens a larger sequence of genealogies and registers that connects the restored community with Adam, Israel’s tribes, David, priests, Levites, and preexilic Jerusalem. Read in that wider setting, the names trace an expansive line from humanity’s beginnings through Abraham and Israel, while also including other family lines. The genealogy therefore frames Israel’s identity within a larger inherited story.","verse_refs":["1 Chronicles 1:1-54"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_chronicles_001_0db65fd7c30fb0ae99f09265"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Genealogies That Follow","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The genealogies in the following chapters are selective literary maps of kinship, land, office, and restored identity, rather than exhaustive pedigrees in a modern sense. Differences such as gaps, parallel lines, variant names, or compressed generations therefore need to be considered case by case.","verse_refs":["1 Chronicles 2:1-55","1 Chronicles 3:1-24","1 Chronicles 4:1-43","1 Chronicles 5:1-26","1 Chronicles 6:1-81","1 Chronicles 7:1-40","1 Chronicles 8:1-40","1 Chronicles 9:1-44"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_chronicles_001_56f760274b826af44998b6b6"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}